      "description": "Applied Investing and Allocation puts students in the seat of an institutional allocator. Each student takes on an allocator type (sovereign wealth fund, pension fund, endowment or foundation, insurance company, or family office) and works through the decisions that allocator faces today across public equities, fixed income, private markets, private credit, and real assets. Sessions are organized around six dimensions every allocator must weigh: time horizon, liquidity, return, risk, tax, and expense. Throughout the course, students apply a Signal vs. Noise Diagnostic to separate durable structural shifts from short-term market narratives, working from primary sources such as allocator annual reports, GP letters, and industry data. Senior practitioners from across allocator types join as guest speakers. Coursework includes weekly group assignments, an individual Investment Journal, and a final investment memo presented in an Investment Committee format.",
